    assistant: "This is a common and important modeling question. The answer is a junction table (also called a join table or bridge table) with extra columns.\n\n**Example: Users enrolled in Courses with enrollment metadata**\n\n```sql\n-- The entities\nCREATE TABLE users (\n  id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),\n  name TEXT NOT NULL,\n  email TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE courses (\n  id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),\n  title TEXT NOT NULL,\n  max_capacity INT NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- The junction table WITH metadata\nCREATE TABLE enrollments (\n  user_id UUID REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n  course_id UUID REFERENCES courses(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n  enrolled_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW(),\n  role TEXT DEFAULT 'student' CHECK (role IN ('student', 'ta', 'auditor')),\n  grade TEXT,\n  completed BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE,\n  PRIMARY KEY (user_id, course_id)\n);\n\n-- Indexes for both directions\nCREATE INDEX idx_enrollments_course ON enrollments(course_id);\n-- user_id is already indexed via the primary key\n```\n\n**Querying:**\n```sql\n-- User's courses with enrollment details\nSELECT c.title, e.role, e.grade, e.enrolled_at\nFROM enrollments e\nJOIN courses c ON c.id = e.course_id\nWHERE e.user_id = $1\nORDER BY e.enrolled_at DESC;\n\n-- Course roster\nSELECT u.name, u.email, e.role, e.grade\nFROM enrollments e\nJOIN users u ON u.id = e.user_id\nWHERE e.course_id = $1;\n```\n\n**In an ORM (Prisma example):**\n```prisma\nmodel Enrollment {\n  userId     String\n  courseId   String\n  enrolledAt DateTime @default(now())\n  role       String   @default(\"student\")\n  grade      String?\n  completed  Boolean  @default(false)\n\n  user   User   @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id])\n  course Course @relation(fields: [courseId], references: [id])\n\n  @@id([userId, courseId])\n}\n```\n\nThe junction table is its own first-class entity when it carries metadata. Think of it as \"the enrollment\" rather than just \"the link between user and course.\" That mental model helps when the metadata grows."
