    assistant: "This works but it's sequential — each `await` blocks until the previous one completes. For 1000 users, if each call takes 100ms, that's 100 seconds total.\n\n**The fix: concurrent execution with `asyncio.gather`**\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom typing import List\n\nasync def fetch_all_users() -> List[User]:\n    tasks = [fetch_user(user_id) for user_id in range(1000)]\n    return await asyncio.gather(*tasks)\n```\n\nNow all 1000 requests run concurrently. Same 100ms per call, but total time drops to roughly 100ms (plus overhead).\n\n**But wait — 1000 concurrent requests might overwhelm the target service.** Use a semaphore:\n\n```python\nasync def fetch_all_users(concurrency: int = 50) -> List[User]:\n    semaphore = asyncio.Semaphore(concurrency)\n    \n    async def bounded_fetch(user_id: int) -> User:\n        async with semaphore:\n            return await fetch_user(user_id)\n    \n    tasks = [bounded_fetch(uid) for uid in range(1000)]\n    return await asyncio.gather(*tasks)\n```\n\nThis runs up to 50 requests at a time — fast but respectful. Add error handling with `return_exceptions=True` if individual failures shouldn't crash the whole batch.\n\nThe sequential version was correct — just slow. Concurrency is where async really shines."
