    user: "How do I write good unit tests?",
    assistant: "Good unit tests follow a pattern I call AAA: Arrange, Act, Assert.\n\n```ts\n// Testing a price calculator\ndescribe('calculateTotal', () => {\n  // Test name describes the behavior, not the implementation\n  it('applies percentage discount to subtotal', () => {\n    // Arrange: set up the inputs\n    const items = [\n      { name: 'Widget', price: 100, quantity: 2 },\n      { name: 'Gadget', price: 50, quantity: 1 },\n    ];\n    const discount = { type: 'percentage', value: 10 };\n\n    // Act: call the function\n    const total = calculateTotal(items, discount);\n\n    // Assert: verify the output\n    expect(total).toBe(225); // (200 + 50) * 0.9\n  });\n\n  it('returns subtotal when no discount is provided', () => {\n    const items = [{ name: 'Widget', price: 100, quantity: 1 }];\n    \n    const total = calculateTotal(items);\n    \n    expect(total).toBe(100);\n  });\n\n  it('throws on negative prices', () => {\n    const items = [{ name: 'Widget', price: -10, quantity: 1 }];\n    \n    expect(() => calculateTotal(items)).toThrow('Price must be non-negative');\n  });\n\n  it('returns 0 for empty cart', () => {\n    expect(calculateTotal([])).toBe(0);\n  });\n});\n```\n\n**Principles of good unit tests:**\n\n1. **Test behavior, not implementation.** Don't test that an internal method was called — test that the output is correct.\n\n2. **One assertion per concept.** Each test checks one thing. Multiple assertions are fine if they verify the same concept.\n\n3. **Descriptive names.** Someone reading the test should understand what the function does without reading the code.\n\n4. **Cover edge cases:** empty input, zero values, negative numbers, null/undefined, boundary values.\n\n5. **Tests are documentation.** A well-written test suite tells the reader exactly how the function behaves.\n\n6. **Fast.** Unit tests should run in milliseconds. If they need a database or network, they're integration tests.\n\nWhat are you testing? I can help you identify the right test cases."
